mx-auto">Are more serious example of NoSql injection is when a special quer=
y operators are used, such as =E2=80=98$where=E2=80=99 or =E2=80=98$exists=
=E2=80=99 (as well as others). As you know these are also seen as php varia=
bles, this may make it possible for attackers to insert code into the query=
. One way to do this is through HTTP Parameter Pollution (we will go into t=
his in another post).</p><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto=
">The PHP documentation for MongoDB warns against this:</p><p class=3D"mb-4=
 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto">Please make sure that for all special q=
uery operators (starting with $) you use single quotes so that PHP doesn=E2=
=80=99t try to replace =E2=80=9C$exists=E2=80=9D with the value of the vari=
able $exists.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto">With M=
ongoDB you can use something like<strong> </strong>mongo-sanitize to guard =
against potential injection attacks. Of course you should still validate yo=
ur user input yourself before relying on a third party. &nbsp;</p><h3 class=
=3D"text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl  my-8 text-left max-w-[1120px] mx-au=
to">But What if You Are Not Using MongoDB?</h3><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  =
max-w-[1120px] mx-auto">Again the answer is simple, validate and sanitise y=
our data. PHP has many ways to check user input, for example if you are req=
uesting and expecting a user to submit an email address you could use Sanit=
ize Filters to handle this for you as well as other methods. </p><p class=
=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto">If you are using DynamoDB where=
 injection does not look to be easy to achieve, it does not mean that you c=
an simply store the user input without any validation. This stored unvalida=
ted/unsanatised data could <a class=3D"text-blue-500 hover:underline" href=
=3D"https://worknest.com/blog/second-order-sql-injection-part-3-of-the-sql-=
series" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">lead to a Second Orde=
r SQL Injection</a>.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto"=
>Second Order SQL Injection is a vulnerability that occurs when submitted v=
alues are stored in a database and used at another function in the applicat=
ion. Again we can talk about this in more detail at another time. </p><p cl=
ass=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto">Let=E2=80=99s think about ho=
w a NoSQL database often work alongside SQL databases. So if a user manages=
 to store a =E2=80=981=3D1=E2=80=99 into a NoSQL table, and that item is pu=
lled back into the application and processed by SQL this can be a big issue=
. </p><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto">Keep in mind as w=
ell that NoSQL databases are often used to store large amounts of data quic=
kly that can come in from third party. How do you know that the third party=
 API feed or CSV has not been compromised itself? The answer is you don=E2=
=80=99t. Therefor again do not rely on a third party to make sure your appl=
ication is safe. Validate! Validate! Validate! </p><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb=
-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto">SQL injection is still one of the most common v=
ulnerabilities we come across. With NoSQL databases becoming more and more =
popular, the number of NoSQL injection attacks is also likely to rise. Beca=
use of this, it is vital that you as a developer do not forget the basics w=
hen it comes with security. Always sanitise and validate your user input da=
ta. Likewise if user data is going to be used in another part of the applic=
ation make sure you validate the output data from one part of the applicati=
on before it is used in another.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120=
px] mx-auto"></p><p class=3D"mb-4 last:mb-0  max-w-[1120px] mx-auto"></p></=
